-central-1.amazonaws.com/UNK.png"}] "UNK"]]] [:tr {} [:th {:valign "top"} "Details"] [:td {} "Gates: 15" [:br {}] "Length: 3461 m" [:br {}] "Start Altitude: 2282 m" [:br {}] "Vertical Drop: 902 m"]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "This was the last Alpine event at the Cortina Olympics and Austria’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/97080"} "Toni Sailer"] " came into it having already won the slalom and giant slalom. Sailer expected to win, as he held the course record for the Olimpia run on Tofana. But only a few minutes before he started, Sailer broke a strap on his boots. Aided by Italian team trainer " [:a {:href "/athletes/97431"} "Hans Senger"] ", Sailer was able to fix his boot in time to start. Going off as the 14th starter, the leader at the time was Germany’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/89413"} "Hans Peter Lanig"] ", but Sailer bested his time by 7.6 seconds. It led to an easy victory for him as only three skiers came within five seconds of his time, with Switzerland’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/85671"} "Raymond Fellay"] " second and Sailer’s teammate, " [:a {:href "/athletes/95538"} "Anderl Molterer"] ", third. Acclaimed the greatest Alpine skier ever, Sailer succeeded " [:a {:href "/athletes/85544"} "Stein Eriksen"] " as the sport’s superstar. In 1958 at the World Championships in Bad Gastein, he would win three of the four gold medals, failing only in the slalom where he took silver. He then turned professional and used his movie-star good looks to spend several years as an actor and singer."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Time"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "14"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/97080"} "Toni Sailer"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/AUT"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/AUT.png"}] 